          "type": "turn",
          "question": "From what age does guidance describe screening for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "According to the National Diabetes Association Standards of Care and Preventive Services Guidance, all adults should be screened for type 2 diabetes starting at age 35.\n\nYounger adults who are overweight or obese and have additional risk factors should also be screened. These risk factors include a first-degree relative with diabetes, a high-risk ethnicity, established cardiovascular disease, high blood pressure, low HDL cholesterol or raised triglycerides, polycystic ovary syndrome, physical inactivity, or a history of gestational diabetes. For people of Asian ancestry, a lower body-mass-index threshold applies.\n\nIf screening results are normal, repeat screening is usually recommended every three years, or more frequently if risk factors accumulate or weight increases.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
